{"name":"Jewellery White","hex":"#ced6e6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jewellery","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Collectively, personal ornamentation such as rings, necklaces, brooches and bracelets, made of precious metals and sometimes set with gemstones.","Any tools, instruments, devices, etc., placed within a drill pipe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jewellery"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jewellery","blocks":[{"text":"Jewellery consists of decorative items worn for personal adornment such as brooches, rings, necklaces, earrings, pendants, bracelets, and cufflinks. Jewellery may be attached to the body or the clothes. From a Western perspective, the term is restricted to durable ornaments, excluding flowers for example. For many centuries, metals such as gold and silver, often combined with gemstones, have been the standard material for jewellery. Other materials such as glass, shells, or wood may also be used."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Pectoral_and_Necklace_of_Sithathoryunet_with_the_Name_of_Senwosret_II_MET_DT531.jpg/330px-Pectoral_and_Necklace_of_Sithathoryunet_with_the_Name_of_Senwosret_II_MET_DT531.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewellery","wikidata":"Q161439"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Reconstructed_sumerian_headgear_necklaces_british_museum.JPG/500px-Reconstructed_sumerian_headgear_necklaces_british_museum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Reconstructed_sumerian_headgear_necklaces_british_museum.JPG/1280px-Reconstructed_sumerian_headgear_necklaces_british_museum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Reconstructed_sumerian_headgear_necklaces_british_museum.JPG/500px-Reconstructed_sumerian_headgear_necklaces_british_museum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReconstructed_sumerian_headgear_necklaces_british_museum.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Golden_bracelets_with_snakes_at_the_National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Athens_on_1_June_2018.jpg/500px-Golden_bracelets_with_snakes_at_the_National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Athens_on_1_June_2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Golden_bracelets_with_snakes_at_the_National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Athens_on_1_June_2018.jpg/1280px-Gold